{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:57:17Z","timestamp":1790625437279,"version":"4.1.0"},"reference-count":39,"publisher":"Noyam Publishers","issue":"8","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,26]],"date-time":"2026-09-26T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1790380800000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["ERATS"],"abstract":"<jats:p>This study examined the pervasive issue of corruption within secular and religious institutions, aiming to understand how these systems, which ought to uphold justice and virtue, can be manipulated for selfish interests. The study used a doctrinal approach to review the literature and explore the motivations behind corruption in both legal and faith-based organisations. The findings reveal a significant disconnect between the ideals of law and faith-based actions, a total dichotomy from societal norms and natural justice. Additionally, legal loopholes are frequently exploited, allowing unethical behaviour to thrive, while religious leaders sometimes misuse their authority, betraying the trust of their congregations. Corruption erodes public trust in institutions designed to serve society, confidence, and the willingness to question. This growing disillusionment can lead to apathy toward civic responsibilities, diminished participation in democratic processes, and a culture of cynicism that undermines social cohesion and stability. Furthermore, the paper highlighted the need for reforms focused on ethical leadership to restore integrity. The study recommends fostering a culture of accountability to bridge the gap between the principles of law and faith and their applications. Contributions of this study to scholarship include innovative mechanisms to eradicate corruption in both secular and religious contexts, strengthened oversight, the implementation of educational reforms that promote trust and accountability, and the reestablishment of boni mores in the broader society. Essentially, the paper evokes the fundamental principles that cultivate transformed systems that enforce change rather than serving as vehicles of corruption.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.38159\/erats.20261284","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T19:11:11Z","timestamp":1790622671000},"page":"376","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.38159\/p2020084","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["The irony of law and faith in corruption"],"prefix":"10.38159","volume":"12","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9725-8910","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Norless Zibele","family":"Nodangala","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/02svzjn28","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Walter Sisulu University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0212-2856","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zimkhitha","family":"Ngqeleni","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/02svzjn28","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Walter Sisulu University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"25245","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,26]]},"reference":[{"key":"ref0","unstructured":"American Bar Association.  Trust and Confidence in the U.S. Justice System, 2022."},{"key":"ref1","unstructured":"Brown, T., and P. Nkosi. \u201cCorruption and Governance in South Africa: An Overview.\u201d Journal of Politics in Africa, 2023."},{"key":"ref2","unstructured":"Davis, R. Faith Betrayed: The Crisis of Leadership in Religious Institutions. Religious Studies Review, 2021."},{"key":"ref3","unstructured":"Department of Public Service and Administration. Annual Report 2022\/2